      The new #include <sbi/sbi_tlb.h> provides the declaration for __sbi_sfence_vma_all(), which is a trivial inline-asm wrapper around the sfence.vma instruction. Neither this header nor the function it declares involves vector registers, vector arguments, the V extension, or any fixed-length vector calling convention. The ABI_VLEN mismatch vulnerability described in the spec requires compilation units that pass fixed-length vector arguments across calls — this header and call site have zero vector-typed parameters. Security spec is inapplicable to this code.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:130-151  switch_to_next_domain_context
      The PR inserts __sbi_sfence_vma_all() at line 140 immediately after csr_swap(CSR_SATP, ...) at line 139. This is a TLB consistency fix: when the SATP CSR is swapped to a new domain context whose satp.MODE may differ (e.g., Sv39 vs Sv48), stale TLB entries from the prior address-width mode must be flushed to prevent hardware from misinterpreting cached translations. The sfence.vma instruction achieves this. None of the code in this region touches vector registers (v0-v31), uses vector loads/stores, or passes fixed-length vector arguments across any call boundary. The CSR swap sequence operates exclusively on scalar supervisor CSRs (sstatus, sie, stvec, sscratch, sepc, scause, stval, sip, satp, scounteren, senvcfg, srmcfg). The trap context save/restore at lines 149-151 copies struct sbi_trap_context (GPRs + mepc + mstatus), which also contains no vector state. The ABI_VLEN vulnerability pattern — mismatched vector register allocation across compilation units — is categorically inapplicable to this code path.

